      "accordion_parsys5": {
        "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
        "sling:resourceType": "wcm/foundation/components/parsys",
        "accPosId": 5,
        "cols": {
          "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
          "jcr:createdBy": "user-b3eaac8b1c@example.invalid",
          "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "user-b3eaac8b1c@example.invalid",
          "jcr:created": "Wed Feb 19 2025 22:33:49 GMT+0000",
          "jcr:lastModified": "Wed Feb 19 2025 22:33:49 GMT+0000",
          "sling:resourceType": "stryker/components/content/columncontrol/cols",
          "col1": {
            "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
            "sling:resourceType": "wcm/foundation/components/parsys",
            "text_copy_copy": {
              "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
              "jcr:createdBy": "user-1f15d09fdf@example.invalid",
              "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "user-b3eaac8b1c@example.invalid",
              "jcr:created": "Thu Feb 06 2025 18:32:06 GMT+0000",
              "text": "<p><span class=\"futura-bold\">BACKGROUND</span><br>\nFollowing&nbsp;retromastoid&nbsp;craniectomy&nbsp;for microvascular decompression of the fifth or seventh&nbsp;cranial&nbsp;nerve, the preferred method and value of&nbsp;cranioplasty&nbsp;remains disputed.<br>\n</p>\n<p><span class=\"futura-bold\">METHODS</span><br>\nIn this study, we report the functional outcome of&nbsp;calcium&nbsp;phosphate&nbsp;cranioplasty&nbsp;following&nbsp;first-time microvascular decompression in 79 consecutive patients who underwent operations over a one-year period.<br>\n</p>\n<p><span class=\"futura-bold\">RESULTS</span><br>\nNo patient experienced a deep infection, cerebrospinal fluid leak or undue incisional pain at long-term follow-up. Additionally, all patients stated that they were satisfied with the cosmetic outcome.<br>\n</p>\n<p><span class=\"futura-bold\">DISCUSSION</span><br>\nAlthough this technique is unlikely to affect the rates of infection and postoperative pain, we believe that the low rate of CSF leak provides a unique advantage over other currently used methods of closing&nbsp;retromastoid&nbsp;craniectomies.<br>\n</p>\n",
              "jcr:lastModified": "Wed Feb 19 2025 22:31:34 GMT+0000",
              "sling:resourceType": "stryker/components/content/text",
              "textIsRich": "true"
            },
            "curatedcta_copy_copy": {
              "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
              "jcr:createdBy": "user-1f15d09fdf@example.invalid",
              "CTAOpenInNewTab": "true",
              "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "user-b3eaac8b1c@example.invalid",
              "ctaLinkType": "ext",
              "ctaOpenInNewTabOverride": "0",
              "jcr:created": "Thu Feb 06 2025 18:34:55 GMT+0000",
              "CTATitle": "Full Article",
              "ctaExternalLink": "http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/?term=The+utility+of+calcium+phosphate+cement+in+cranioplasty+following+retromastoid+craniectomy+for+cranial+neuralgias",
              "jcr:lastModified": "Wed Feb 19 2025 22:31:54 GMT+0000",
              "sling:resourceType": "stryker/components/content/curatedcta",
              "CTAButtonColor": "rgb(133,69,138)"
            }
          }
        }
      },
      "accordion_parsys6": {
        "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
        "sling:resourceType": "wcm/foundation/components/parsys",
        "accPosId": 6,
        "cols": {
          "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
          "jcr:createdBy": "user-b3eaac8b1c@example.invalid",
          "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "user-b3eaac8b1c@example.invalid",
          "jcr:created": "Wed Feb 19 2025 22:33:59 GMT+0000",
          "jcr:lastModified": "Wed Feb 19 2025 22:33:59 GMT+0000",
          "sling:resourceType": "stryker/components/content/columncontrol/cols",
          "col1": {
            "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
            "sling:resourceType": "wcm/foundation/components/parsys",
            "text_copy_copy_copy": {
              "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
              "jcr:createdBy": "user-1f15d09fdf@example.invalid",
              "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "user-b3eaac8b1c@example.invalid",
              "jcr:created": "Thu Feb 06 2025 18:32:06 GMT+0000",
              "text": "<p>We report a novel technique for closure using titanium mesh cranioplasty in addition to hydroxyapatite cement and abdominal fat graft for acoustic neuroma. We reviewed 15 patients who underwent translabyrinthine craniectomy for resection of acoustic neuroma. Hearing loss was documented prior to surgical procedure. Over 2 years, patients underwent titanium mesh and hydroxyapatite cranioplasty with abdominal fat graft. Participants included seven men and eight women, age range 38 to 65. Main outcome measures included cosmetic outcome and incidence of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) leak. The lesion was right-sided in seven patients and left-sided in eight. Cosmetic outcome was excellent in all. There were no cases of CSF leak. Closure used one-third the hydroxyapatite required for traditional closure. Our technique yields cosmetic results equivalent to hydroxyapatite cement alone and a comparable incidence of CSF leakage without leaving a drain in place postoperatively. The technique is easy to adopt, is more cost-effective than hydroxyapatite cement cranioplasty alone, offers greater ease of access for reoperation, and does not preclude later implantation of bone-anchored hearing aid.<br>\n</p>\n",
              "jcr:lastModified": "Wed Feb 19 2025 22:32:27 GMT+0000",
              "sling:resourceType": "stryker/components/content/text",
              "textIsRich": "true"
            },
            "curatedcta_copy_copy": {
